\description{
The \verb{rdev_*()} functions run \verb{R CMD check} on a set of CRAN packages with
two builds of R and report the differences. The typical use is testing a
local change to R itself: build R once from the unmodified sources (the
\emph{old} build) and once with your change applied (the \emph{new} build), then
check the packages most likely to be affected. Packages that pass with the
old build and fail with the new one point at regressions caused by the
change.

This reuses the \code{\link[=revdep_check]{revdep_check()}} machinery, with the two R builds taking
the place of the CRAN and development versions of a package. Results and
reports have the same shape.
}
\details{
A check is driven from a \emph{root} directory, created by \code{rdev_init()}. It
holds the configuration (\code{rdev.yml}), the results database, the package
libraries and check output, and the reports. The usual workflow is:
\enumerate{
\item \code{rdev_init()} records the paths of the two R binaries.
\item \code{rdev_add()} selects the packages to check: an explicit list, the
reverse dependencies of some packages, or both.
\item \code{rdev_check()} installs the dependencies of each package into a
private library, checks it with both builds, and writes the reports
(\code{README.md}, \code{problems.md} and \code{failures.md}) to the root.
